# Light and dark mode (required)

Every new or changed **UI/UX** surface must support both **light** and **dark** themes. Do not ship pages, components, or screens that only look correct in one mode.

## Required

- Theme toggle (or system preference + optional override) available in the relevant shell/layout
- Persist user preference (e.g. `localStorage` / session / user setting)
- Use theme tokens / CSS variables (or Tailwind `dark:` variants) — not hard-coded one-mode colors
- Text, borders, icons, charts, maps, and images must remain readable and on-brand in both modes
- Respect `prefers-color-scheme` when no saved preference exists

## Avoid

- Hard-coded hex/rgb backgrounds or text that break in the other mode
- Light-only or dark-only mockups treated as “done”
- Mixing ad-hoc colors that ignore the shared theme tokens

## When building or changing UI

1. Implement (or reuse) the shared theme system first.
2. Style with semantic tokens (`bg-background`, `text-foreground`, brand accents) or equivalent project variables.
3. Verify contrast and hierarchy in **both** light and dark before finishing.

Applies to web Blade/JS, Flutter apps, admin/vendor/customer portals, and marketing pages unless the user explicitly exempts a surface.
